WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.529
We've seen countless King villains, some are
utterly horrifying and others are completely

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:06.529
laughable.

00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:10.350
But there's a handful that are so terrifying
they'll inspire nightmares.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:14.450
These are the Stephen King movie villains
who've kept us up at night, ranked from the

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:15.660
worst to the very best.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:16.660
Warden Samuel Norton

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:20.480
The Shawshank Redemption demonstrates how
some of the scariest movie monsters are the

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.340
ones you could meet in real life.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:26.670
Warden Samuel Norton is a man who's willing
to do whatever it takes to gain power over

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:27.670
others.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:30.940
"Don't you ever mention money to me again."

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:36.370
He claims to be a man of God, but he runs
Shawshank State Prison like a slave owner,

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:40.550
manipulating inmates to do his bidding and
punishing anyone who doesn't comply.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:45.239
Ultimately, Norton is a coward: He chooses
to take the easy way out when confronted with

00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:46.510
his own misdeeds.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:51.500
But cowards like Norton are capable of doing
some truly frightening and despicable things,

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:52.680
and they shouldn't be underestimated.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:53.680
Reverend Lowe

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.420
We can't say for certain whether Stephen King
has something against organized religion,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.580
but religious figures are often the villains
in his story.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:05.199
Take Reverend Lowe for example, a man of the
cloth with a beastly secret.

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:10.369
1985's Silver Bullet tells the story of a
small town terrorized by a werewolf and the

00:01:10.369 --> 00:01:13.340
people who are brave enough to fight the ungodly
creature.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:17.250
The film is indicative of a lot of horror
films that came out in the 1980s, it's more

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.249
cheesy than actually scary.

00:01:19.249 --> 00:01:23.039
Honestly, the fact that Gary Busey is one
of the stars tells you everything you need

00:01:23.039 --> 00:01:24.039
to know.

00:01:24.039 --> 00:01:28.799
Still, werewolves are scary beasts, and the
fact that this werewolf is the town's religious

00:01:28.799 --> 00:01:30.689
leader makes him all the more frightening.

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:31.689
Isaac Chromer

00:01:31.689 --> 00:01:36.439
There's nothing scarier than an evil child,
and the town in Children of the Corn is full

00:01:36.439 --> 00:01:37.439
of them.

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:42.209
Some might argue that the scariest presence
in the film is He Who Walks Behind the Rows.

00:01:42.209 --> 00:01:46.869
That's the fertility demon who dwells in the
cornfield, and these nasty kids keep sacrificing

00:01:46.869 --> 00:01:49.079
the town's adults in order to appease him.

00:01:49.079 --> 00:01:53.969
Of course, demons are bad news on the best
of days…but there's something about a charismatic

00:01:53.969 --> 00:01:58.579
cult leader, a child in this case, that strikes
us as even more horrifying.

00:01:58.579 --> 00:02:04.159
"In the dream, the Lord did come to me, and
he was a shape.

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:07.579
It was He Who Walks Behind The Rows."

00:02:07.579 --> 00:02:12.160
Actor John Franklin does an incredible job
portraying a corrupt, power-mad adolescent.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.700
Thanks to his performance, the film becomes
less about the horror of a cornfield demon

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:20.670
and more about the dangers of charismatic
leaders with frightening ulterior motives.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:21.670
Christine

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:22.670
Beep, beep!

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:26.590
What's worse than buying a new car only to
find out it's pure evil?

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:28.319
Uh…nothing, actually.

00:02:28.319 --> 00:02:33.150
Christine is a unique horror film that takes
something as mundane as a car and turns it

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:35.500
into an unstoppable killing machine.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:40.380
Christine isn't one of the most popular King
adaptations, but it's still a pretty intense

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:45.180
thrill ride, and the film is certainly worth
giving a whirl.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.760
"Okay.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:49.400
Show me."

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:55.210
While it's obviously impossible for an inanimate
object to convey emotion, director John Carpenter

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:59.940
and his star Keith Gordon are great at making
it seem like Christine has a personality all

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:03.040
her own…however terrifying that personality
may be.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.520
Fun fact: The film's famous resurrection scene
almost didn't happen on screen.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:11.430
Carpenter added it after production wrapped
because he felt the movie needed more special

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:12.430
effects.

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:13.430
Cujo

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:16.800
Stephen King is an absolute master when it
comes to turning life's everyday comforts

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:18.460
into total nightmares.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:23.690
Cujo tells the story of Donna and Tad, a mother
and son who are forced into a hot, broken-down

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:28.069
car by a rabid St. Bernard that's quite literally
foaming at the mouth.

00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:32.310
The film turns out to be less about a killer
dog and more about human relationships and

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:36.870
childhood trauma, and Cujo comes to represent
all the terrible things that are happening

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.069
in Donna's family.

00:03:38.069 --> 00:03:42.830
Also, what's more frightening than watching
a lovable creature turn into a bloodthirsty

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:44.900
monster, and through no fault of his own?

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:49.370
At the beginning of the film, Cujo is a sweet,
curious dog who simply winds up in the wrong

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:51.060
place at the wrong time.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:04:01.850
Oh, and speaking of being in the wrong place
at the wrong time:

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:05.400
Throughout the film, the audience feels a
real sense of helplessness as they watch the

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:10.430
creature transform before their eyes…and
that sense of helplessness soon turns to utter

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:11.430
horror.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:12.430
Margaret White

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:14.880
Mothers can be monstrous, in real life, and
in the movies.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:19.699
And as far as bad mamas go, you'd be hard
pressed to find a mother as depraved and dangerous

00:04:19.699 --> 00:04:21.789
as Carrie's Margaret White.

00:04:21.789 --> 00:04:26.249
She's a religious zealot who feels an overwhelming
compulsion to punish her daughter Carrie at

00:04:26.249 --> 00:04:29.660
every turn, calling her a "sinner" whenever
she gets the chance.

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.969
She's also fond of locking poor Carrie in
a prayer closet, essentially a broom closet

00:04:33.969 --> 00:04:38.740
adorned with candles and religious icons,
including the creepiest crucifix you've ever

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:39.740
seen.

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:44.050
When Carrie gets her first period, Margaret
tells her it's because she's had impure thoughts.

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:49.419
"Oh Lord, help the sinning woman see the sin
of her days and ways!"

00:04:49.419 --> 00:04:53.870
At the end of the day, she's an all-around
miserable person who thinks sex and sexuality

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:55.240
is sinful and revolting.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:05:00.279
Of course, Margaret gets hers in the end...because
having a telekinetic daughter who throws knives

00:05:00.279 --> 00:05:02.539
with her mind isn't without its risks.

00:05:02.539 --> 00:05:03.889
But while she's alive?

00:05:03.889 --> 00:05:06.130
Man, Margaret is a friggin' nightmare.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:07.130
Kurt Barlow

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:10.360
Kurt Barlow from Salem's Lot is the quintessential
boogeyman.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:14.229
The vampire is barely seen throughout the
two-part miniseries, which had a theatrical

00:05:14.229 --> 00:05:18.470
release in Europe, preferring to prey on the
people of Salem's Lot under a veil of darkness

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:19.949
and chic mystique.

00:05:19.949 --> 00:05:25.319
But when he finally does show his face, he's
a decidedly unsexy vampire, there's no mistaking

00:05:25.319 --> 00:05:26.999
him for Edward Cullen.

00:05:26.999 --> 00:05:31.720
"I'm going to kill you!"

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:37.090
Barlow is more akin to Nosferatu's central
creep, another repulsive monster who doesn't

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:39.069
waste time trying to charm his victims.

00:05:39.069 --> 00:05:43.040
Barlow keeps his eye on the prize and just
focuses on what he does best: Terrorizing

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:44.900
the townspeople.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:53.100
"Don't look at him!"

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:53.740
Gage Creed

00:05:53.759 --> 00:05:58.000
When he was alive, Pet Sematary's Gage Creed
was a real cute little kid.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:02.979
But after a fatal accident involving an 18-wheeler,
his father made the perfect worst choice and

00:06:02.979 --> 00:06:05.439
decided to go ahead and reanimate him.

00:06:05.439 --> 00:06:09.840
The result of all this inadvisable hocus pocus
is…not so cute.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.870
"I brought you something mommy."

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:15.250
Okay, maybe he's still pretty cute.

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:19.100
Nevertheless, he turns into a murderous zombie
with a fondness for scalpels.

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:20.100
He sneaks.

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:21.100
He creeps.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:23.340
You'll always find him in the most unexpected
places.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:26.650
Basically, he's a full-fledged menace who
must be stopped!

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:31.789
In the end, Gage's odd mixture of childlike
innocence and unstoppable evil adds up to

00:06:31.789 --> 00:06:33.870
pure, unadulterated nightmare fuel.

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:36.050
As a wise man once said:

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:39.349
"Sometimes dead is better."

00:06:39.349 --> 00:06:41.750
Gage is the embodiment of corrupted innocence.

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:46.660
A victim of unfortunate circumstances, his
transformation into a tiny monster is ultimately

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:48.990
the byproduct of his father's grief.

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:55.469
In that regard, Pet Sematary is a terribly
sad film, on top of being absolutely terrifying.

00:06:55.469 --> 00:06:56.469
The Mist monsters

00:06:56.469 --> 00:07:00.300
Let's face it: Most insects and arachnids
are pretty off-putting in real life.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:04.490
The mere sight of a creepy-crawly is a surefire
way to get the blood pumping, a fact that

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:07.039
horror directors are all too happy to exploit.

00:07:07.039 --> 00:07:12.699
Well, the creatures in The Mist are all super-sized…and
so is your mounting sense of dread.

00:07:12.699 --> 00:07:16.759
Just take a look at this angry militia of
creepos if you don't believe us.

00:07:16.759 --> 00:07:21.360
Most of the action in The Mist takes place
in a single location: A store that's suddenly

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:24.569
become enveloped in a thick, mysterious fog.

00:07:24.569 --> 00:07:27.539
Most of the people trapped inside are loath
to venture out.

00:07:27.539 --> 00:07:31.600
Those who do explore the mist end up meeting
horrible fates…

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:36.199
The mist monsters are otherworldly creatures
that go sight unseen throughout much of the

00:07:36.199 --> 00:07:37.199
film.

00:07:37.199 --> 00:07:49.409
But when we finally do catch a glimpse, it's
not a happy sight.

00:07:49.409 --> 00:07:52.789
Those oversized tentacles are not for the
faint of heart.

00:07:52.789 --> 00:07:55.599
And the sharp pinchers should give you pause,
too.

00:07:55.599 --> 00:07:59.470
They're ghastly, malevolent monsters, and
it's really best to avoid them.

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:01.410
There's certainly no reasoning with them.

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:10.860
Just try talking some sense into this guy.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:13.559
These monsters are seemingly unstoppable,
too.

00:08:13.559 --> 00:08:17.840
The only way to save yourself from a gruesome
death is to fight back, and that's way easier

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:18.860
said than done.

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:19.860
Annie Wilkes

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:21.439
Looking for a life of fame and glory?

00:08:21.439 --> 00:08:24.710
Well, Annie Wilkes will make you think twice
about that.

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:29.050
Misery tells the story of Paul Sheldon, a
famous writer who's taken in by a seemingly

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:32.219
gregarious nurse following a serious car accident.

00:08:32.219 --> 00:08:36.010
Annie is all-too willing to help Paul get
back on his feet, but she's also happy to

00:08:36.010 --> 00:08:37.430
break his ankles.

00:08:37.430 --> 00:08:41.539
After all, she's his Number One Fan, and she
feels things very strongly.

00:08:41.539 --> 00:08:44.400
"I'm also a nurse."

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:48.050
And in case you haven't caught on yet, she's
also a total friggin' psychopath.

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:52.570
"There, look there, see what you made me do?"

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:57.150
Wilkes holds the poor writer hostage, forcing
him to rewrite his latest book in a way that

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:58.790
she deems satisfying and appropriate.

00:08:58.790 --> 00:08:59.790
No profanity.

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:00.790
No cheap endings.

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:06.330
And when he tries to escape this horrible
predicament, she hobbles him with a sledgehammer,

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:09.430
all the while assuring him that she's his
"biggest fan."

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:13.820
Annie is easily one of Stephen King's most
frightening villains, and part of what makes

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:17.370
her so terrifying is Kathy Bates' incredible
performance.

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:21.700
She rightfully earned an Oscar for her work,
and it's easy to see why Total Film said,

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:25.510
"[Annie Wilkes is] one of cinema's greatest,
friendliest, monsters."

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:30.330
Indeed, part of what makes Annie so awful
is the fact that she really does love Paul.

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:34.220
But when admiration turns into obsession,
there's no telling what a person is capable

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:38.810
of doing, and Annie is apparently willing
to do anything, no matter how wretched it

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:39.810
is.

00:09:39.810 --> 00:09:40.810
The Overlook Hotel

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:44.880
The 1980 film adaptation of The Shining is
a bonafide horror masterpiece.

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:49.520
Director Stanley Kubrick created a ghostly
and ghastly world inside The Overlook Hotel...and

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:54.830
his utterly terrifying film is widely considered
an essential, iconic piece of horror cinema.

00:09:54.830 --> 00:10:00.380
The story revolves around a writer and family
man who loses his mind inside a haunted hotel,

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:02.650
which sounds somewhat straightforward at first.

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:04.300
But there's a lot going on here.

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:08.340
Though Kubrick's vision is something of a
departure from Stephen King's original novel,

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:13.200
his take on the haunted hotel has unspeakable
horrors waiting behind every door, and just

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:14.380
around every corner.

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:19.200
As the story goes, The Overlook Hotel was
apparently built on an Indian burial ground,

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:22.330
and any horror fan knows that's never a good
sign.

00:10:22.330 --> 00:10:26.000
Throughout the film, the formidable building
establishes itself as something of a villain

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:31.070
in its own right, it really feels like a living,
breathing entity with blood on its mind.

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:35.320
The ghosts that seem to inhabit the property
are all deranged and diabolical in one way

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:36.320
or another.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:37.890
There are elevators full of blood.

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:40.390
Ballrooms teeming with ghostly spectres.

00:10:40.390 --> 00:10:54.200
And who the hell knows what's going on in
Room 237?

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:58.930
From the Grady twins to these two fine fellows,
there's something to fear on every floor of

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:00.110
The Overlook Hotel.

00:11:00.110 --> 00:11:02.240
You can run, but you can't hide.

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:03.240
Jack Torrance

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:07.800
Though the spectral hotel guests at the Overlook
Hotel are admittedly creepy, it's questionable

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:10.290
whether any of them can physically harm you.

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:14.350
Still, there's no doubt they'll drive you
towards madness using every trick at their

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:15.350
disposal.

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:18.890
Case in point: Jack Torrance, who totally
loses his mind throughout the course of the

00:11:18.890 --> 00:11:19.890
film.

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:23.730
He starts out as a deeply frustrated husband
and father who seems to have a rather short

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:28.530
fuse...but he slowly transforms into a genuine
monster, a monster who happens to be highly

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:31.950
proficient with an axe.

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:36.330
"Here's Johnny!"

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:39.270
Taking the job at the Overlook proves to be
Jack's undoing.

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:43.750
We watch helplessly as he loses his grip on
reality, and sometimes it feels like we're

00:11:43.750 --> 00:11:45.320
losing it right along with him.

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:50.040
He becomes more and more terrifying with every
scene…and when he finally meets his demise,

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:54.450
there's something truly awful in that frozen
grimace, and the vision stays with us long

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:56.150
after the final frame of the film.

00:11:56.150 --> 00:11:59.190
Of course, that final frame is creepy in its
own right.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:00.190
Pennywise

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:04.420
If you were a child in the '90s, there's a
good chance you caught the It miniseries starring

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:07.860
Tim Curry as the child-eating Pennywise the
Dancing Clown.

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:08.910
"C'mon, Bucko."

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:13.120
"I'm not supposed to take stuff from strangers.

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:15.220
My dad said so."

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:17.770
"Very wise of your dad."

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:21.730
Curry's Pennywise was a villain that made
kids afraid to turn off their bedroom lights,

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:25.920
not just because clowns are inherently awful,
but because this clown can manifest as your

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:27.160
greatest fear.

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:30.370
He can also get to you quite easily…wherever
you happen to be.

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:35.350
Although the It miniseries has arguably lost
a bit of its bite in recent years, Pennywise

00:12:35.350 --> 00:12:38.340
the Dancing Clown is still a force to be reckoned
with.

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:43.180
In the recent It films, Bill Skarsgård takes
on the meaty role, and his interpretation

00:12:43.180 --> 00:12:45.870
is dazzlingly demented in its own right.

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:46.880
The pancake makeup.

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:47.940
The teeth.

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:59.340
That hideous dance!

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:02.300
The new Pennywise is the perfect villain for
these troubling times.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:05.910
At the end of the day, we all get the monsters
we deserve.

